 - How you say a line: select words in the message box and pick from the toolbar that appears (rises, louder, softer, whispered, slower, a pause, breathy, smiling, a breath in, angry…), or press ♪ beside the box for the word at the cursor. The marks stay out of sight: the box shows your words, the marked ones softly underlined, and the chat sets your line the way it sounds. The engine reads every mark exactly: a whispered or shouted line carries as far as that voice does, and the listener hears your feeling right as often as people do, sometimes hearing another.
 - "Say it another way" puts your line back in the box, marks and all, and takes it out of the chat; the next line stands in its place.
 - People placed on the map overhear what is said near them, as far as the voice carries to each, and remember what they make out.
-- Code writes calls for your character's own acts: in "Tom punches Mara" or "I tell Will to wait an hour" the other person's act is read from the scene; "she" in a line written in the third person is your character when the line names no one else she could be. Acts the line says are not done ("I don't hit him", "I almost fall") and out-of-character asides ("(OOC: …)") are not acted on. Typed ›guillemets‹ are quotation marks, and capitals read as louder on common words only (STOP, NOOOO; NATO and ICU stay as written, and so do the WHO and the IT desk). You can speak to a group as to a person ("I shout to the crew", "I tell the town council"), and to someone named with their looks ("the old blind beggar").
+- Code writes calls for your character's own acts: in "Tom punches Mara" or "I tell Will to wait an hour" the other person's act is read from the scene; "she" in a line written in the third person is your character when the line names no one else she could be. Acts the line says are not done ("I don't hit him", "I almost fall") and out-of-character asides ("(OOC: …)") are not acted on. Typed ›guillemets‹, «guillemets» and „German quotes“ are quotation marks, read as speech like "…", and capitals read as louder on common words only (STOP, NOOOO; NATO and ICU stay as written, and so do the WHO and the IT desk). You can speak to a group as to a person ("I shout to the crew", "I tell the town council"), and to someone named with their looks ("the old blind beggar").
 - A line in the third person ("Mara climbs the wall") or in asterisks is read by its verbs like one in the first person, and words in bold or italics ("I punch **Tom**", "I _whisper_ to Will") are read as the words; a line that opens "No, I …" or "No, Will is …" is an ordinary line, and only a short "no, Will" or "I meant …" redoes the last turn. Regenerating after a reply that failed or was stopped, or pressing Send with the box empty, runs the turn once; a line edited first runs as edited. Deleting a reply keeps what your line did: the line stays in the chat. Each line keeps the world it started from, so a swipe, a regenerate or a "no, Will" starts from the line itself, also after messages before or after it are deleted. Curly apostrophes read as straight ones ("I’ll punch him"). "I run" runs at the pace runners choose, "I dash" sprints and "I hurry" walks as fast as you can walk; "5 m to the door, then 10 m to the gate" is walked in both legs.
 - A result that ends "Fragile: …" rests on a judged value: within that value's range the outcome comes out otherwise. A room emptying through one door takes a few seconds to work out, the reruns for its judged values included.
 
